Coverage of the first-year essentials:
- company registration;
- work permits under Instructions No. 1 of 2026;
- the federal 80/20 staffing ratio (Council of Ministers, 40th session, 8/10/2024);
- social security under Law 18/2023;
- bilingual contracts.
Also ask how the firm follows matters outside Baghdad, including the Kurdistan Region, which applies Law 71/1987 and a 75% local-staff rule.

Under Law 18/2023:
- employer social security of 12% (25% for private and mixed-sector employers whose profits come from selling Iraqi oil and hydrocarbons);
- plus the State's 8% share for foreign workers who are subject to Iraqi social security;
- plus an IQD 2,000,000 fee that repeats with every permit renewal.
Add the end-of-service gratuity of two weeks' wage per year (Art. 45), which is payable in all cases, including resignation.

Usually yes, because recurring obligations start after registration:
- yearly permit renewals for each foreign employee, with the IQD 2,000,000 fee repeating each time;
- monthly social-security contributions;
- monitoring the Labour Law amendment, which is still only at first reading (25/7/2026).

Either, in different matters. At Al-Taswia we act for both employees and employers in Iraqi labour law, our specialist area, including:
- dismissals;
- end-of-service pay, which is payable in all cases, including resignation;
- social security;
- foreign work permits.

Yes. Al-Taswia Legal Services Company's leading area is Iraqi labour law, and it also handles:
- civil and banking law;
- criminal cases and company registration;
- review of Iraqi legislation and Arabic–English legal translation.
The office is in Al-Yarmouk, Baghdad. Phone +964 771 100 2200.

Al-Taswia works with companies in:
- oil and gas, insurance and banking;
- contracting, telecoms and IT;
- aviation, pharmaceuticals and hospitals.
It acts for Iraqi and foreign companies operating in Iraq, with English-language communication, and covers matters across Iraq from its only office, in Baghdad.

Ask:
- which areas the firm covers itself (registration, employment, contracts, banking, criminal exposure, legislative monitoring, translation);
- who the named lawyer is;
- the language of reports;
- how it covers sites outside Baghdad, including the Kurdistan Region.

Not as-is. Contracts in federal Iraq must respect the minimums of Labour Law No. 37 of 2015, for example:
- probation once only, up to 3 months (Art. 37);
- the rule on fixed-term renewals (Art. 38);
- 30 days' notice (Art. 44);
- the gratuity, which is due in all cases (Art. 45).
A foreign worker's contract must also be in Arabic and English (Art. 9, Instructions No. 1 of 2026).

It should contain:
- the work permit with its issue and expiry dates (one year, renewable: Art. 5, Instructions No. 1 of 2026);
- the Arabic–English contract (Art. 9);
- proof that the IQD 2,000,000 fee was paid at each renewal;
- the social-security record.
